  {
    question: 'What\'s the difference between mAh and Wh?',
    answer: '<p>Both measure battery capacity, but differently:</p><ul><li><strong>mAh (milliamp-hours):</strong> Measures charge at nominal voltage (usually 3.7V)</li><li><strong>Wh (watt-hours):</strong> Measures total energy (voltage × capacity)</li></ul><p><strong>Conversion formula:</strong> Wh = mAh × V / 1000</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> 20,000mAh × 3.7V / 1000 = 74Wh</p><p>Airlines use Wh for regulations (100Wh limit). Most power banks list mAh on packaging and Wh in specs.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How long do power banks last?',
    answer: '<p><strong>Most quality power banks last 3-5 years</strong> with proper care. Factors affecting lifespan:</p><ul><li><strong>Charge cycles:</strong> Most batteries handle 500+ full cycles</li><li><strong>Heat exposure:</strong> Avoid leaving in hot cars or direct sunlight</li><li><strong>Storage charge:</strong> Store at 50-80% charge, not empty or 100%</li><li><strong>Quality:</strong> Branded batteries (Anker, Mophie) outlast no-name brands</li></ul><p><strong>Signs of aging:</strong> Reduced capacity, slower charging, overheating. Replace when capacity noticeably drops.</p>'
