  <!-- Laptop Charging Requirements -->
  <h2 id="laptop-charging">Laptop Charging Requirements</h2>
  <p>
    Not all power banks can charge laptops. Here's what you need:
  </p>

  <h3>Minimum Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>USB-C PD (Power Delivery):</strong> Required protocol for laptop charging</li>
    <li><strong>45W minimum output:</strong> Most laptops need at least 45W to charge while off</li>
    <li><strong>65W recommended:</strong> Charges most ultrabooks at full speed</li>
    <li><strong>100W+ for power laptops:</strong> 16" MacBook Pro, gaming laptops, etc.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Laptop Power Requirements (Common Models)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MacBook Air M3:</strong> 30W (any USB-C PD charger works)</li>
    <li><strong>MacBook Pro 14":</strong> 70W (charges slower with less)</li>
    <li><strong>MacBook Pro 16":</strong> 140W (Anker 737 recommended)</li>
    <li><strong>Dell XPS 13:</strong> 45-65W</li>
    <li><strong>ThinkPad X1 Carbon:</strong> 65W</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- TSA Regulations -->
  <h2 id="tsa-regulations">TSA & Airline Power Bank Regulations</h2>
  <p>
    Understanding regulations prevents airport hassles:
  </p>

  <h3>Key Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Carry-on only:</strong> Power banks are NEVER allowed in checked bags</li>
    <li><strong>Under 100Wh:</strong> Allowed without airline approval</li>
    <li><strong>100-160Wh:</strong> Requires airline approval (usually granted)</li>
    <li><strong>Over 160Wh:</strong> Generally prohibited</li>
    <li><strong>Quantity:</strong> Most airlines allow 2 per passenger</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Converting mAh to Wh</h3>
  <p>
    Formula: <strong>Wh = mAh × 3.7V / 1000</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>10,000mAh = 37Wh (always allowed)</li>
    <li>20,000mAh = 74Wh (always allowed)</li>
    <li>26,800mAh = 99.16Wh (within limit)</li>
    <li>27,000mAh = 99.9Wh (within limit)</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Fast Charging -->
  <h2 id="fast-charging">Fast Charging Standards Explained</h2>
  <p>
    Multiple fast charging standards exist. Here's what matters:
  </p>

  <h3>USB-C PD (Power Delivery)</h3>
  <p>
    The universal standard. Works with iPhones, Android, laptops, tablets. Ranges from 18W to 240W.
    <strong>This is what you want for versatility.</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>Quick Charge (Qualcomm)</h3>
  <p>
    Common on Android devices. QC 3.0 delivers up to 18W, QC 4+ up to 27W. Compatible with many USB-C PD chargers.
  </p>

  <h3>Apple MagSafe</h3>
  <p>
    Magnetic wireless charging for iPhone 12+. Up to 15W with certified chargers, 7.5W with most third-party options.
  </p>

  <h3>Our Recommendation</h3>
  <p>
    Prioritize <strong>USB-C PD</strong> for maximum compatibility. It works with everything and provides the
    fastest wired charging for most devices.
  </p>

